description:
Genspark is an all-in-one AI workspace offering generative AI tools for writing, image generation, video creation, design, coding, and business applications. Designed to automate repetitive professional tasks, Genspark.ai transforms simple text instructions into concrete deliverables, such as presentations, spreadsheets, or applications.
Founded by former Silicon Valley executives (from Google, Microsoft, and Meta), Genspark stands out by acting as a true "productivity operating system." Instead of forcing users to juggle multiple subscriptions, it dynamically integrates and orchestrates the best models on the market (such as GPT-5, Claude 4.5, or Gemini) based on specific needs. The platform is built on Genspark 6.0, launched in the summer of 2026, which introduces a shared memory system ("Second Brain") and mixed human-agent teams ("Gen Team").
Its major tools include:

AI Slides: Generates complete professional presentations (design and sourced content) from a single prompt;
AI Sheets: Creates automated databases and financial models using natural language;
AI Developer: Enables coding and deploying full websites or applications without writing a single line of code;
AI Inbox: Connects to email inboxes to synthesize threads and draft contextual responses.

Founded in 2023 and later restructured under a unified global entity in early 2025 by Jonathan Ahdoot (CEO), the company is headquartered in Montreal, Canada, and New York, USA. A major pivot event marked its history: its acquisition by the Hypertec Cloud technology group, merging expertise in high-density data centers and high-performance computing (HPC) systems to create the current leader in "AI Factories." Today, 5C manages a massive portfolio of data center pipelines totaling over 1.5 to 2 gigawatts (GW) of energy capacity dedicated to next-generation graphics processing units (GPUs).
Its business model is based on selling raw computing capacity (AI Cloud) on a pay-per-use basis or through long-term subscriptions for AI research labs, as well as leasing dedicated spaces (Colocation Data Center) for enterprise clients. The startup takes its name from its five fundamental pillars dedicated to frontier AI architectures (Frontier Labs):
Customization: Tailor-made design of hardware and cooling systems;
Celerity: Ultra-fast deployment of computing servers;
Cutting Edge: Direct-to-chip liquid cooling, essential for overheating AI chips;
Capacity: Guaranteed access to electrical power and terabyte networks;
Climate: Energy optimization to reduce the carbon footprint of large language model (LLM) factories.

Elasticsearch, developed by Elastic (listed on the NYSE under the symbol NYSE: ESTC), is the world's most popular distributed, open-source search and data analytics engine. Written in Java and based on the Apache Lucene library, it enables the exploration, storage, and analysis of vast volumes of data (text, numbers, logs) in near real-time.
Elasticsearch is the cornerstone of an essential technological ecosystem known as the Elastic Suite, historically referred to as the ELK Suite. This suite includes:
Elasticsearch, which serves as a JSON document-oriented database and indexing engine;
Logstash, the data ingestion tool that collects, filters, and transforms data before sending it to Elasticsearch;
Kibana, the graphical visualization interface that allows the creation of interactive dashboards from Elasticsearch data;
and Beats, lightweight agents installed on servers to directly send data (metrics, logs) to the suite.
To remain the leader in document analysis in the era of generative artificial intelligence, Elasticsearch has undergone a major architectural transformation. It has natively integrated vector storage (embeddings), essential for large language model (LLM) technologies, and has established itself as one of the most widely used knowledge databases for providing ultra-precise business context to AI applications, such as ChatGPT or Claude, through the RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) method.
